windowGcT -- windows stroke 0 (SEQ 747, 2026-11-22), F-153: does an open window hide our globals from the collector?
A delta of resizeLightT (copied, then patched): the same two resizes of firstLight through redrawWindow, run under an open
offscreen window with one compiled action alive across them. Rows in genLadder/formsPop.sh, which runs it at GCSTRESS=1000.

WG-1  windowRootProbe before any window. Four sets of 8 collectable blocks: held only by globals of this image in bss,
      by globals in data, only by hidden pointers (the known-bad end), by an uncollectable array (the known-good end).
      It prints how many of each set a collection kept: 8, 8, 0, 8.
WG-2  the same probe under the window, after GC_set_no_dls(1): the globals still keep 8 of 8. This is F-153's question.
WG-3  testing(wgCount) compiles wgCount and fires it under the window: wgN 3. (testing, not jitter;: see below.)
      Then resizeLightT's rows, unchanged: two relayouts and four pages. formsPop compares the pages and points with
      resizeLightT's own from the same run, by value.
WG-4  jitRefire() fires the compiled code again after the relayouts and their collections: wgN 6.
WG-5  one more collection and the probe again: as WG-2.

THE CONTROL (formsPop runs it): WINDOW_CLEAR_ROOTS=1 drops every static root when the window opens and roots only the
controller, which is the state F-153 described. The probe then reads 0 of 8 kept for both sets of globals. Measured 2026-11-22: in
that state the relayouts, the pages and WG-4 still read right, at GCSTRESS=1000 and at 20 -- so the relayout run cannot see
a hidden global and only the probe rows can. The relayout rows say a window run survives; the probe rows say why.

WHY NOT jitter; -- with jitter; on and NO window, setFrame compiles and then refuses every relayout ("the form firstLight
holds itself"). That is the jitter's, not the window's or the collector's (docs/fixIts.md F-167). wgCount is compiled alone.
